---

<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  category="Buying"
  published={published}
  dateModified={dateModified}
  readTime={readTime}
  slug={slug}
  breadcrumbLabel="AI Agent Pricing Index"
  exploreCategories={["Frameworks", "Enterprise", "No-Code", "Coding", "Workflow"]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <!-- 1. Introduction -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="introduction" class="guide-h2">
      Why AI agent pricing is hard to compare
    </h2>
    <p>
      Vendor pages optimize for a clean story: “$20/seat,” “free tier,”
      “usage-based.” AI agents do not bill like simple SaaS seats. They{" "}
      <strong>loop</strong> — plan, call tools, retry, re-read context — so the
      same sticker price can produce a 5–10× bill once real multi-step work starts.
    </p>
    <p>Comparisons fail when buyers:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Treat free tiers as production economics</li>
      <li>Ignore model tokens (or assume “included” means unlimited)</li>
      <li>Omit builder, maintainer, and review labor</li>
      <li>Skip failure cost and compliance time</li>
      <li>Call open-source “free” without infra and eng load</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>What this index covers:</strong> how platforms charge, a category-by-category
      pricing index (coding, frameworks, no-code, enterprise, workflow), scannable
      comparison tables, hidden costs, and a forecasting framework. It is an{" "}
      <strong>index of cost models and ranges</strong>, not a live price list for
      every SKU on earth.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How to use it:</strong> start with the model that matches your
      shortlist, read that category section, map one golden path into billable
      units, then run the estimate framework. Pair with our{" "}
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> (Value is one of six scores) and
      head-to-head pricing sections linked throughout.
    </p>
    <InfoCallout title="Data updated: August 2026" variant="info">
      <p>
        Public list prices move. Where we cite numbers (e.g. Claude Pro/Max,
        Cursor Pro ~$20), treat them as{" "}
        <strong>planning signals</strong>, not quotes. Opaque enterprise and
        credit products are described by <em>drivers</em>, not invented line
        items. Always verify on vendor sites before you buy.
      </p>
    </InfoCallout>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Key takeaway -->
  <section id="key-takeaway">
    <h2 class="guide-h2">Key takeaway</h2>
    <KeyTakeaway>
      In 2026, the winning metric is{" "}
      <strong>cost per successful outcome at 3× pilot volume</strong> — seats +
      meters + model tokens + people + failure risk — not free-tier sticker price.
      If two platforms are close on software, pick the one your team will actually
      maintain without five overlapping tools.
    </KeyTakeaway>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. How they charge -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="how-they-charge" class="guide-h2">
      How AI agent platforms charge
    </h2>
    <p>
      Most products mix these models. Identify the primary meter before you compare
      “price.”
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose mt-8 space-y-4">
      {
        pricingModels.map((m) => (
          <div class="card-surface p-5 sm:p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">{m.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted sm:text-[0.9375rem]">
              {m.body}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
              <strong class="text-ink">Gets expensive when:</strong> {m.expensive}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <InfoCallout title="Full TCO still has more layers" variant="tip">
      <p>
        Beyond the invoice: infrastructure, observability, HITL staffing, and
        opportunity cost of wrong actions. If you only model seats + credits, you
        are still shopping marketing.
      </p>
    </InfoCallout>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. Pricing index by category -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="pricing-index" class="guide-h2">Pricing index by category</h2>
    <p>
      Use this as a map of <strong>typical models, entry bands, and cost drivers</strong>.
      Deep product math lives in the linked guides.
    </p>

    {
      categories.map((cat) => (
        <div class="mt-12" id={cat.id}>
          <h3 class="guide-h3">{cat.title}</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">Examples: {cat.examples}</p>
          <div class="not-prose mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="card-surface p-4 sm:p-5">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-soft">
                Typical pricing model
              </p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{cat.model}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card-surface p-4 sm:p-5">
              <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-soft">
                Entry cost (planning)
              </p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{cat.entry}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">What drives cost up</p>
          <ul class="mt-2">
            {cat.drivers.map((d) => (
              <li>{d}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
            <strong class="text-ink">Best for:</strong> {cat.bestFor}
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-2 text-sm">
            {cat.links.map((l) => (
              <a href={l.href} class="font-semibold text-accent hover:text-accent-hover">
                {l.label} →
              </a>
            ))}
          </p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <!-- 5. Side-by-side tables -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="comparison-tables" class="guide-h2">
      Side-by-side pricing comparison tables
    </h2>
    <p>
      Scannable planning tables. Exact dollars change; drivers are more stable.
    </p>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Category overview</h3>
    <ComparisonTable
      caption="AI agent platform pricing shapes (2026 planning index)"
      headers={["Typical model", "Entry / common band", "Primary cost driver"]}
      rows={categoryOverviewRows}
    />

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Coding agents (illustrative public signals)</h3>
    <ComparisonTable
      caption="Coding agent cost shape — verify on vendor sites"
      headers={["Claude Code", "Cursor", "OpenAI Codex", "Cline (BYO)"]}
      rows={codingCompareRows}
      highlightIndex={0}
    />
    <p class="text-sm text-ink-soft">
      Claude detail:{" "}
      <a href="/guides/claude-code/pricing/">Claude Code pricing guide</a>.
      Product fit:{" "}
      <a href="/guides/claude-code-vs-cursor/">vs Cursor</a>,{" "}
      <a href="/guides/claude-code/vs-codex/">vs Codex</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 class="guide-h3">Workflow automation</h3>
    <ComparisonTable
      caption="Workflow / automation agent cost shape"
      headers={["n8n", "Make", "Zapier Agents / Zapier"]}
      rows={workflowCompareRows}
    />

    <h3 class="guide-h3">No-code vs frameworks vs enterprise (honest opacity)</h3>
    <ComparisonTable
      caption="Where public list price ends"
      headers={["No-code builders", "OSS frameworks", "Enterprise suites"]}
      rows={[
        {
          label: "What you see on the website",
          cells: [
            "Plans + credits; often clear until volume",
            "Free/OSS + API pricing elsewhere",
            "Limited public SKUs; sales-led packages",
          ],
        },
        {
          label: "What you must model yourself",
          cells: [
            "Credits per multi-step agent run",
            "Eng weeks + tokens + tracing",
            "Capacity, SI, CoE, suite bundles",
          ],
        },
        {
          label: "Buyer risk if you only compare stickers",
          cells: [
            "Surprise credit burn at automation scale",
            "Underestimating people cost by 10×",
            "Signing ACV before a golden path works",
          ],
        },
      ]}
    />

    <p class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Representative profiles</p>
    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="n8n"
      badge="Workflow"
      blurb="Self-host free + model APIs, or Cloud execution fees — classic SMB automation cost shape."
    />
    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="cursor"
      badge="Coding seats"
      blurb="Predictable seats with agent-intensity overages — model heavy weeks, not free-tier demos."
    />
    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="microsoft-copilot-studio"
      badge="Enterprise"
      blurb="Marginal cost of suite agents vs greenfield vendors — procurement and CoE often dominate year one."
    />
    <ToolReviewCard
      slug="langgraph"
      badge="Framework"
      blurb="Control and multi-agent graphs — software free, production cost in eng + models + ops."
    />
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Hidden costs -->
  <section>
    <h2 id="hidden-costs" class="guide-h2">Hidden costs buyers miss</h2>
    <ul class="not-prose mt-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {
